Approach 5 — DFS Left-to-Right + Overwrite

Another useful recursive variation is to traverse:

left → right

and overwrite the answer for a depth.

Because the right subtree is visited later, the rightmost node replaces the earlier node.

Code
class Solution {

    public List<Integer> rightSideView(TreeNode root) {

        List<Integer> answer = new ArrayList<>();

        dfs(root, 0, answer);

        return answer;
    }

    private void dfs(
            TreeNode root,
            int depth,
            List<Integer> answer) {

        if (root == null) {
            return;
        }

        if (depth == answer.size()) {
            answer.add(root.val);
        } else {
            answer.set(depth, root.val);
        }

        dfs(root.left, depth + 1, answer);
        dfs(root.right, depth + 1, answer);
    }
}
Why this works

For:

        1
       / \
      2   3

At level 1:

visit 2
answer = [1,2]

Then:

visit 3
answer = [1,3]

The right node overwrites the left node.

However, the right-first DFS is easier to understand:

first node at each level = answer

So I recommend that one.

Complexity Comparison
Approach	Technique	Time	Extra Space	Practical
1	Repeated DFS for every level	O(nh), worst O(n²)	O(h)	❌
2	DFS right → left, first per level	O(n)	O(h)	⭐ Excellent
3	BFS left → right, last per level	O(n)	O(w)	⭐ Excellent
4	BFS right → left, first per level	O(n)	O(w)	⭐ Excellent
5	DFS left → right, overwrite	O(n)	O(h)	Good

n = number of nodes
h = tree height
w = maximum width

